[{:db/id -1
  :adr/id "ADR-2608122400"
  :adr/title "41 orphan checkouts: 37 hold nothing, 4 hold something, 0 unknown"
  :adr/status "accepted"
  :adr/date "2026-08-12"
  :adr/supersedes ""
  :adr/superseded-by ""
  :adr/body
  "## What was measured

A rename usually removes the old west entry but leaves the old DIRECTORY on
disk. West will never update or recreate those, so they drift quietly and are
invisible to anything that reads `manifest/west.yml`. 39 were identified; the
audit found 41, because one bare name in the list matched two on-disk
directories.

**Nothing was deleted.** This records what deleting would cost, so the decision
can be made on evidence rather than on the word `orphan`.

## Result

- ~~37 SAFE~~ -> **32 SAFE**
- ~~4 HOLD WORK~~ -> **9 HOLD WORK**
- **0 UNKNOWN**

**Corrected 2026-08-12, after the archiving pass.** The audit asked `git stash
list` and answered correctly. It did not ask about `.git/stash-archive-*/`, and
those are the OPPOSITE of a stash: they are patches that earlier cleanup passes
deliberately exported so the work could not be lost. They are plain files under
`.git/`, so no git command reports them and the audit never saw them.

12 of the 37 carry archive directories; **5 carry at least one non-empty patch**,
21 patches in total:

| orphan | non-empty patches | bytes |
|---|---|---|
| `gftdcojp/ai-gftd-mangaka` | 6 | ~145 MB |
| `gftdcojp/cloud-manimani` | 4 | 83,580 |
| `gftdcojp/ai-gftd-dougaka` | 4 | 76,460 |
| `kotoba-lang/kotoba-rad` | 4 | 25,756 |
| `kotoba-lang/kotoba-git` | 3 | 17,955 |

Four of the five registered siblings hold **zero** archives, so that content
exists only in the orphan. The fifth looked like a duplicate -- `kotoba-git` and
`bonsai` have archives at three matching relative paths -- but comparing by
`(archive directory, filename)` rather than by filename alone shows all three
**DIFFER**. Each checkout archived its own working state on those dates. So all
five hold unique content.

A first comparison here got this wrong in the safe direction and is worth
recording: every one of those files is named `tracked-modifications.patch`, so
matching on basename picked the same sibling file three times and reported three
differences that were really one. Name collisions across dated directories make
basename matching meaningless, and it fails loudly here only by luck.

The remaining 7 of the 12 hold only `index.txt` files with no patch content, so
their verdict is unchanged.

**Deleting an orphan destroys its `.git/`, and with it any archive inside.** The
mechanism that exists to make deletion recoverable is itself deleted by the
deletion.

Two facts hold across all 41, and both are stronger than expected:

- **Zero unpushed commits anywhere.** Every local branch tip in every orphan --
  including all 33 in `local-murakumo`, 9 in `cloud-murakumo`, 8 in `kawaraban`
  -- was confirmed present server-side in the registered repository. The only
  commits absent from GitHub are three stash commits.
- **Every orphan HEAD is an ancestor of its registered repo's `main`**, 41 of
  41, with `behind_by = 0`. None diverged.

**All 41 pairs share a GitHub repository id**, so every one is a rename
redirect. That had to be checked rather than assumed: a shared root commit is
also what a FORK has, and two pairs elsewhere in this fleet turned out to be
genuinely different repositories -- deleting either would have destroyed a real
project.

## The method matters, because the obvious one gives a false positive

`git log --branches --not --remotes` is the natural way to ask for unpushed
commits, and **it is wrong here**: most of these clones have zero
remote-tracking refs, so that command reports their ENTIRE history as unpushed.
Every branch tip and stash commit was instead tested two ways -- ancestry in the
sibling's local object database, and existence server-side via
`gh api repos/<registered>/commits/<sha>`.

Dirty working trees were checked file by file with `shasum` rather than by
count. All but one are build output (`.cpcache`, `.shadow-cljs`, `__pycache__`)
or bytes identical to the sibling's worktree or the registered repo's `main`.

## The four that hold something

1. **`gftdcojp/local-murakumo`, `stash@{0}`** (`7d827047`, absent from GitHub).
   `worker.cljs` +6/-2: `auth-ok?` extended to accept a second, separately
   rotatable `BABINIKU_PROXY_TOKEN` beside `ANTHROPIC_PROXY_TOKEN`. The stash
   message itself says drop-forbidden.

   **Correction (measured during archiving): `absent` is literally true and
   materially misleading.** The token NAME appears nowhere in
   `network-awai/local-murakumo` and `auth-ok?` no longer exists -- but the
   CAPABILITY has landed under a different name. `authorize!` now accepts
   `ANTHROPIC_PROXY_TOKEN` and an optional `ANTHROPIC_PROXY_TOKEN_2`, a second
   separately rotatable token, through a pure decision namespace
   `local-murakumo.messages-auth`, plus mk1 capability tokens (ADR-2608011400).

   So the stash is plausibly FUNCTIONALLY SUPERSEDED, and what is unique to it
   is the BabiNiku-specific token name rather than the idea. Read as originally
   written, this entry would have sent someone to port a second-token mechanism
   onto a file that already has one. A grep for the identifier answered
   `is this string here`, not `is this capability here`, and only the second
   question was the one being asked.
2. **`gftdcojp/cloud-murakumo`, `stash@{0}`** (`32dfb542`, absent from GitHub).
   `deps.edn` +3/-1: pins `ed25519` by `:git/url` + `:git/sha` instead of
   `:local/root`. Registered checkout and remote `main` still carry the
   `:local/root` form, so this never landed.
3. **`gftdcojp/shinshi-growth-actor`, `stash@{0}`** (`10c5a441`, absent from
   GitHub). Two untracked blobs -- an earlier `.cljc`/js-fetch variant targeting
   `com.etzhayyim.apps.shinshi-growth.socialPost`. The registered repo has a
   superseding `.clj` port, and the two commits the stash message names as
   superseding it DO exist there. The verdict is conservative on purpose: the
   bytes exist nowhere else, even though the author annotated them as broken.
4. **`gftdcojp/ai-gftd-ongakuka`, untracked `artifacts/`** -- 164 MB, twelve
   rendered audio files the registered sibling does not have. But the sibling
   has the matching `*_input_params.json`, and the registered repo's
   `.gitignore` excludes `artifacts/`, `*.wav`, `*.mp3` -- so this was never
   meant for git and is regenerable output, not lost work.

## Archiving: DONE for all three (2026-08-12)

Each is exported to `.git/stash-archive-20260812/` in its own orphan checkout,
with an `index.txt` carrying the verification transcript, the reapply command,
the base commit and the disposition note -- so the next person needs nothing but
the file. **Verified rather than asserted**: each patch was applied to a clean
tree and the result hashed against the stashed blob; all four files match.

Two things the archiving surfaced that the audit had not:

- **Item 3's content is in `stash^3`** (the untracked-files parent), so a plain
  `git stash show -p` shows NOTHING. The export is
  `git diff <empty-tree> <sha>^3`. An archive taken the obvious way would have
  been an empty file that looked like a successful archive.
- **Item 2 has a `^3` too, but its tree is empty.** A zero-byte listing is kept
  deliberately, so the emptiness is on the record rather than reading as a
  missing file.

The stashes were NOT dropped and no orphan directory was removed. Whether these
changes get ported is still open, per the dispositions below.

## Dispositions

- **Item 1** -- likely superseded (see the correction above). The open question
  is only whether the BabiNiku-specific token name is still wanted.
- **Item 2** -- confirmed NOT landed. `main` still carries the `:local/root`
  form. Two things for whoever ports it: the stash's `:git/url` names
  `kotoba-lang/ed25519`, which is a rename REDIRECT to
  `kotoba-lang/org-ietf-ed25519`; and the pinned sha `fdcefb9f` is real but
  dated 2026-06-29, so the pin is about six weeks stale as written.
- **Item 3** -- the author's supersession note CHECKS OUT. Both cited commits
  exist in the registered successor and are reachable from `main` with
  `behind_by = 0`. The stashed `aozora.cljc` is the earlier `.cljc`/js-fetch
  variant and exists on no branch anywhere, which is exactly why it needed
  archiving rather than trusting the note.

Incidental: `gftdcojp/local-murakumo` and `gftdcojp/cloud-murakumo` are GitHub
rename redirects to `network-awai/...`, and `gftdcojp/shinshi-growth-actor` to
`cloud-itonami/...`. The orphan checkouts' `origin` URLs still carry the old org
names and resolve only through those redirects.

## Before deleting

Re-resolve each stash by SHA at drop time, since a concurrent session can shift
`stash@{0}`. The archives above mean a drop is now recoverable, which it was
not before.

**And move the 21 archived patches somewhere that survives the deletion.**
Copying them out is cheap and unblocks 5 of the 9; the 145 MB in
`ai-gftd-mangaka` needs a size decision, and per the workspace rule on large
binaries that belongs in B2/DataLad rather than in a `.git/` directory.

## What this correction is an instance of

Twice in one day a measurement was correct about the question it asked and wrong
about the question that mattered. Earlier: a grep proved a token NAME was absent
and was read as proving the CAPABILITY was absent, when the capability had
landed under a different name. Here: `git stash list` proved there were no
further STASHES and was read as proving there was nothing to lose, when what
there was to lose had been deliberately moved out of the stash system precisely
so it would not be lost.

Both share a shape. The tool answers about its own domain, the conclusion is
drawn about a wider one, and the gap is invisible because the measurement is
accurate. What catches it is not a better tool -- it is asking, before quoting a
number, what the number would have missed.

For 4, decide whether 164 MB of regenerable audio is worth keeping. If it is, it
belongs in B2/DataLad (skill `large-binary-datalad`), not in an orphan checkout.

Two notes on the remaining 37: `gftdcojp/dougaka-actor` is **not a git
repository at all** -- two log files from a crashed outer-loop run, 4 KB. And
`gftdcojp/cloud-manimani` carries one stray unstaged DELETION of
`src/cloud_manimani/xrpc.cljs`, a file that still exists on `main` and in the
registered checkout, so the deletion is the local anomaly rather than the file.

## Why this is worth a document rather than a cleanup

The cheap version of this task is `rm -rf` on 41 directories, and it would have
been almost right -- 37 of 41. The three stashes are the reason it is not: they
are the only bytes in this set that exist nowhere else, they are individually
small, and two of them are unlanded changes to authentication and dependency
pinning. `git fsck --unreachable` can sometimes recover a dropped stash, but
that is luck, not a plan.

Recorded also because the same shape recurs: `manifest/cleanup-workflow.edn`
already requires archiving before dropping, and the reason it exists is that
confidence has previously been used as a substitute for checking."}]
